[section:leja Leja quadrature]

[heading Synopsis]

`#include <boost/math/quadrature/leja.hpp>`

   namespace boost{ namespace math{ namespace quadrature{

   template <class Real, class ``__Policy`` = boost::math::policies::policy<> >
   class leja_legendre  // the integral of f over [-1, 1]
   {
   public:
      leja_legendre();

      const std::vector<Real>& abscissa() const;
      const std::vector<Real>& weights(unsigned n) const;
      static constexpr unsigned max_points();

      template <class F>
      auto integrate(F f, Real tolerance = tools::root_epsilon<Real>(),
                     Real* error = nullptr, Real* L1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(f(Real(0)));

      // The integral over [a, b]; leja_legendre and leja_jacobi only.
      template <class F>
      auto integrate(F f, Real a, Real b, Real tolerance = tools::root_epsilon<Real>(),
                     Real* error = nullptr, Real* L1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(f(Real(0)));
   };

   template <class Real, class ``__Policy`` = boost::math::policies::policy<> >
   class leja_hermite;  // the integral of f(x) exp(-x[super 2]) over the real line, with the same members except integrate(f, a, b)

   // The integral of f(x) x[super alpha] exp(-x) over \[0, [infin]), alpha > -1; as leja_hermite, with the constructor
   //    explicit leja_laguerre(Real alpha = 0);
   template <class Real, class ``__Policy`` = boost::math::policies::policy<> >
   class leja_laguerre;

   // The integral of f(x) (1 - x)[super alpha] (1 + x)[super beta] over \[-1, 1\], alpha, beta > -1, with the constructor
   //    leja_jacobi(Real alpha, Real beta);
   template <class Real, class ``__Policy`` = boost::math::policies::policy<> >
   class leja_jacobi;

   }}} // namespaces

[heading Description]

A weighted Leja sequence is built greedily: each new point maximizes [radic]/w/ times the product of its distances to the points
already chosen (Leja; Narayan and Jakeman). `leja_legendre` has the Legendre weight /w/(/x/) = 1 on \[-1, 1\], which gives
the classical Leja points, here in the order 0, -1, 1; `leja_hermite` has /w/(/x/) = exp(-/x/[super 2]), `leja_laguerre`
/w/(/x/) = /x/[super [alpha]] exp(-/x/) on \[0, [infin]), and `leja_jacobi` /w/(/x/) = (1 - /x/)[super [alpha]] (1 + /x/)[super [beta]] on \[-1, 1\].
All start from the mean of /w/, so that the one-point rule is the one-point Gauss rule, exact to degree 1, the natural first level
of a sparse grid. Where an exponent is negative, [radic]/w/ has no maximum, and the points are chosen as if it were 0; the weights
are those of /w/. `leja_jacobi` with [alpha] = [beta] = 0 has the points of `leja_legendre`.
The constructors raise a __domain_error unless the exponents are > -1.
The /n/-point rule uses the first /n/ points and has the weights that make it exact for polynomials of degree /n/ - 1.

The rules are nested: going from /n/ to /n/ + 1 points costs one more evaluation of /f/, where a Gauss rule must start again.
This makes them the usual building block of sparse grids, and lets the adaptive `integrate` add one point at a time.
The price is degree: a Leja rule with /n/ points is exact to degree /n/ - 1, a Gauss rule to 2/n/ - 1.

The points and weights are computed when they are first needed, up to `max_points()`: 1023 for the built-in floating point
types and 127 for others. `weights(n)` returns the /n/ weights of the /n/-point rule, computing it and any smaller ones first,
and raises a __domain_error unless 1 [le] /n/ [le] `max_points()`. `abscissa()` returns the points computed so far, in
the order they were chosen. All members are `const` and safe to call from several threads, which share what any of them
computes, as `tanh_sinh` does; points that another thread is computing read as NaN until it is done.
`example/leja_parallel_assembly.cpp` assembles the Hamiltonian of the anharmonic oscillator /x/[super 4] in 60 Hermite functions
on all cores with one `leja_legendre`, in agreement with the exact entries to 2[times]10[super -15] times the largest: 0.21 s on one core
of an Apple M-series and 0.026 s on ten, once the rule has its points.
`leja_hermite` suits expectations over a normal distribution, such as the mean of the logistic function of /X/ ~ N(1, 1):

   leja_hermite<double> rule;
   double error;
   double mean = rule.integrate([](double x) { return 1 / (1 + std::exp(-1 - std::sqrt(2.0) * x)); }, 1e-10, &error)
               / std::sqrt(boost::math::constants::pi<double>());

takes 105 points for 0.69673467014367874, within 4.6[times]10[super -15] of the true value; the estimate of its error, `error` divided by [radic][pi], is 5.6[times]10[super -12].

When several points maximize the product to within rounding, the leftmost is taken; in the tests float, double, long double and
`cpp_bin_float_50` choose the same points. Some weights are negative, but not by much: up to 100 points the sum of |/w[sub i]/|
is at most 1.32 times the total mass for the exponents tried, but 2.44 times for [alpha] = -0.9, [beta] = 1/2.

`integrate(f, tolerance, error, L1)` evaluates /f/ once at each point in turn, until /n/ [ge] 4 and
|/Q[sub m]/ - /Q[sub m - max(1, m/4)]/| [le] `tolerance` * /L1/ for every /m/ from /n/ - /n//4 to /n/, where /Q[sub n]/ is the /n/-point rule
and /L1/ = [sum] |/w[sub i]/| |/f/(/x[sub i]/)|. If `error` or `L1` are not null they are set to the largest of those differences,
a heuristic estimate of the error, and to /L1/.
If `max_points()` is reached first, its result is returned with its error estimate, as `tanh_sinh` does; no error is raised.
/f/ may return a real or complex value, or a vector of them: any type with `operator[]` and `size()`, such as `std::array` or
`std::vector`, whose values all have the same size. A vector is integrated componentwise, and |/y/| above is its max norm.
`leja_legendre` evaluates /f/ at the endpoints [plusminus]1, and `leja_laguerre` and `leja_jacobi` at an end of the support where the exponent is [le] 0.

`integrate(f, a, b, tolerance, error, L1)` integrates /f/ over \[/a/, /b/\] with `leja_legendre`, and /f/(/x/) (/b/ - /x/)[super [alpha]] (/x/ - /a/)[super [beta]]
with `leja_jacobi`, by the affine map from \[-1, 1\], which also scales the error and /L1/. If /a/ > /b/ the result is minus the
integral over \[/b/, /a/\], with [alpha] still at /b/. Infinite or NaN bounds raise a __domain_error.

[heading Accuracy and cost]

The rules up to /N/ points take O(/N/[super 2]) operations: about 0.3 ms for 64 points, 1 ms for 128,
16 ms for 512 and 60 ms for 1023 in double precision, and 0.4 s for 127 in `cpp_bin_float_50`.
Each rule follows from the last in Newton form, with the integral of the nodal polynomial from a recurrence carried to twice the
working precision: the rules integrate /x/[super /k/] to within 20 /n/ [epsilon] [sum] |/w[sub i]/ /x[sub i]/[super /k/]|, and in double
precision the weights are within an ulp of the largest weight of the same rules in 50 digits, up to 1023 points.
A bounded sum of |/w[sub i]/| does not help integrands that grow like exp(/x/[super 2]) against `leja_hermite`, such as products of Hermite polynomials
of high degree: /L1/ is 10[super 19] times the integral of /h/[sub 40][super 2]. Integrate such products with their Gaussian over
a finite interval instead, as the example does.

[heading References]

* F. Leja, ['Sur certaines suites liées aux ensembles plans et leur application à la représentation conforme], Annales Polonici Mathematici 4 (1957), 8-13.
* L. Reichel, ['Newton interpolation at Leja points], BIT 30 (1990), 332-346, [@https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02017352 doi:10.1007/BF02017352].
* A. Narayan and J. D. Jakeman, ['Adaptive Leja sparse grid constructions for stochastic collocation and high-dimensional approximation], SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 36 (2014), no. 6, A2952-A2983, [@https://doi.org/10.1137/140966368 doi:10.1137/140966368].

[endsect] [/section:leja Leja quadrature]

[section:sparse_grid Sparse grids]

[heading Synopsis]

`#include <boost/math/quadrature/sparse_grid.hpp>`

   namespace boost{ namespace math{ namespace quadrature{

   // Rule: leja_legendre<Real>, leja_hermite<Real>, leja_laguerre<Real> or leja_jacobi<Real>, Real = typename Rule::value_type; f(const std::vector<Real>& x), or f(x) with x a std::array<Real, N>, Eigen::VectorXd or other container with operator[] and size().
   template <class F, class Rule>
   auto sparse_grid(F f, const Rule& rule, unsigned dimension, std::size_t max_calls = 100000,
                    Real tolerance = tools::root_epsilon<Real>(), Real* error = nullptr, Real* L1 = nullptr)
      -> decltype(f(x));

   // Over the box [a[0], b[0]] x ... x [a[d-1], b[d-1]], d = a.size(), for leja_legendre and leja_jacobi; Box has operator[] and size().
   template <class F, class Rule, class Box>
   auto sparse_grid(F f, const Rule& rule, const Box& a, const Box& b, std::size_t max_calls = 100000,
                    Real tolerance = tools::root_epsilon<Real>(), Real* error = nullptr, Real* L1 = nullptr)
      -> decltype(f(x));

   }}} // namespaces

[heading Description]

`sparse_grid` integrates /f/ over \[-1, 1\][super /d/] with `leja_legendre`, or /f/(/x/) exp(-|/x/|[super 2]) over the whole space
with `leja_hermite`, or against the product of the weights of `leja_laguerre` or `leja_jacobi`, by the dimension-adaptive sparse grid of Gerstner and Griebel. Coordinate /i/ at level /k[sub i]/ uses the
first 2/k[sub i]/ - 1 points of the rule, and the grid is the sum of the tensor products of differences of consecutive levels
over a set of multi-indices /k/. The set grows where these contributions are largest, so the grid spends its points on the
coordinates, and the interactions between them, that matter. Exact integrals of products of polynomials of degree 2/k[sub i]/ - 2
need only the multi-index /k/ and those below it, and a function of a few coordinates costs little more than in those dimensions.

The levels add two points at a time, not one: for an even /f/ such as /x/[super 2] the rules with one and two points agree, and a
zero difference would stop the refinement of that coordinate. For the same reason, when the error estimate (the sum of the
norms of the contributions not yet refined) falls below `tolerance` * /L1/, every such contribution is refined once more,
and the result is accepted only if the estimate is still below the tolerance. Nothing is accepted while /L1/ is 0, as it is while /f/ has been
zero at every point: /x/[sub 1][super 2] /x/[sub 2][super 2], with `leja_legendre`, is zero at the first point, the mean of the weight,
and on the axes through it. After /d/ + 1 such rounds of refinement with /f/ still zero everywhere, the result is 0 with an error estimate of 0. An interaction of three or more variables
that vanishes on the planes through the mean can still be missed if /f/ is not zero there, such as 1 + /x/[sub 1][super 2] /x/[sub 2][super 2] /x/[sub 3][super 2]. /L1/ is the grid's estimate of the integral of |/f/| times the
weight, the scale of the tolerance; before the grid has converged it can be far off, even negative. It is not a condition
number: the sparse grid's weights have mixed signs, and the sum of |/w[sub i] f/(/x[sub i]/)| can be much larger.
If `error` or `L1` are not null they are set to these. The grid computes the points of `rule` it needs, and keeps them there for the next call.

`max_calls` bounds the number of evaluations of /f/: a refinement that would exceed it is not made, and the result has an
error estimate above the tolerance. A contribution that would need more points than that in a coordinate is not refined
further, and its norm stays in the error estimate;
if the calls run out during the final refinements the error estimate is NaN. No error is raised. A __domain_error is
raised if `dimension` or `max_calls` is zero, or if /f/ takes a fixed-size container whose size is not `dimension`. /f/ may return a real or complex value or a vector of them, as for the
one-dimensional rules; a vector is integrated componentwise, and its error estimate and /L1/ use the max norm.
With bounds `a` and `b`, each coordinate is mapped as `integrate(f, a[i], b[i])` maps it, and the result, error and /L1/ are scaled by the
product of the factors; a __domain_error is raised if a bound is infinite or NaN or `b.size()` is not `a.size()`.

The bookkeeping costs under 2 microseconds per evaluation of /f/ in double precision on Genz's test integrands, more where
the levels are high, so a sparse grid pays off when /f/ costs more than that. On Genz's test integrands, with 10[super 4] calls or more, it beats
quasi-Monte Carlo (Boost.Random's Sobol' sequence) by orders of magnitude up to four dimensions; in eight it still wins for
entire functions such as cos(0.7 + [sum] /c[sub i] x[sub i]/), but not for peaked ones such as Genz's product peak and Gaussian,
and in sixteen quasi-Monte Carlo is better unless /f/ depends mostly on a few coordinates. For integrands with kinks, such as
exp(-[sum] /c[sub i]/|/x[sub i]/ - /u[sub i]/|), quasi-Monte Carlo is better in any dimension, and the error estimate, a heuristic
as for the one-dimensional rule, can be too small.

[heading References]

* T. Gerstner and M. Griebel, ['Dimension-adaptive tensor-product quadrature], Computing 71 (2003), 65-87, [@https://doi.org/10.1007/s00607-003-0015-5 doi:10.1007/s00607-003-0015-5].
* A. Narayan and J. D. Jakeman, as above.

[endsect] [/section:sparse_grid Sparse grids]
